# 1Password env-file for the PRODUCTION Tier-1 synthetics deploy (GOL-2325).
#
# `op run --env-file=this -- <cmd>` resolves the op:// refs to real secret
# values and injects them; the values never touch shell scrollback, history, or
# the committed repo. This file is COMMITTED — it holds REFERENCES + non-secret
# constants, NOT secrets. Read access to the vault -> you get the values; no
# access -> `op` errors and nothing leaks. (Same pattern as prod-reconfig.env.op.)
#
# The op service account is READ-ONLY (op-service-account memory): it can read
# these refs but cannot create/write items. Any NEW secret — notably the
# least-privilege canary Odoo API key (SYNTHETIC_ODOO_API_KEY) — must be minted
# and put in the vault BY A HUMAN first (Josh). See
# docs/RUNBOOK-prod-synthetics-golive.md for the mint + gate procedure.
#
# Render the deploy's --env-file on the prod Odoo droplet, then bring the stack
# up (setup-monitoring.py seeds the canary before the runner ever fires):
#   op run --env-file=scripts/prod-monitoring.env.op -- \
#     sh -c 'env | grep -E "^(OPENOBSERVE|KEEP|DISCORD|SYNTHETIC|ODOO|GHOST|POSTGRES|COST|OTELCOL|BEYLA)_" \
#            > /etc/grove/.env.monitoring'
#   make monitoring-up MONITORING_COMPOSE=docker-compose.monitoring.app-plane.yml \
#                      MONITORING_ENV_FILE=/etc/grove/.env.monitoring
#
# EVERY op:// ref in this file was verified to resolve against the live vault on
# 2026-09-29 (GOL-2325). Before that sweep five refs pointed at vault fields that
# DO NOT EXIST and two were literal REPLACE_ITEM_ID placeholders; because `op run`
# aborts the whole render on any single unresolvable ref, the prod deploy could
# not have rendered at all. The only remaining placeholders are the two
# checkout-canary refs, which are COMMENTED OUT and gated (see that section).
# If you add a ref, check it with: op read "<the op:// url>"

# ── Image pins (non-secret; keep in sync with release-manifest.yaml) ──────────
OTELCOL_TAG="0.115.0"
BEYLA_TAG="1.9.4"

# ── OpenObserve sink — the grove-obs droplet (cross-plane, GOL-280) ───────────
# Prod ships OTLP to the SAME obs droplet QA uses (spec §1: "existing QA obs
# droplet OpenObserve is fine as the sink"). Point both OTLP bases at grove-obs
# and use its ROOT creds. The runner's env label comes from COST_ENV below.
# GOL-2325 (2026-09-29): these were `op://...Grove Infra/obs_openobserve_otlp_{base,metrics_url}`,
# but NEITHER FIELD EXISTS in the vault — `op run` hard-fails on an unresolvable
# ref, so the whole render died before any journey could run. They are not
# secrets (a host:port), so they are plain constants here.
# grove-obs (583896515) and grove-prod-odoo (601081550) are both nyc3 and share
# VPC 9774e39e-dc84-11e8-80bc-3cfdfea9fba1, so ingest stays on the private
# network and never crosses the public internet in plaintext. 10.132.23.199 is
# grove-obs's private address; ADR-010 makes grove-obs the canonical sink.
OPENOBSERVE_OTLP_BASE="http://10.132.23.199:5080/api/default"
OPENOBSERVE_OTLP_METRICS_URL="http://10.132.23.199:5080/api/default/v1/metrics"
OPENOBSERVE_ROOT_EMAIL="op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/obs_openobserve_root_email"
OPENOBSERVE_ROOT_PASSWORD="op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/obs_openobserve_root_password"

# Metric env label — tags every synthetic/USE/cost metric so prod & qa streams
# are distinguishable in OpenObserve. MUST be "prod" for this deploy.
COST_ENV="prod"

# ── Synthetic runner targeting (non-secret) ──────────────────────────────────
# On the prod Odoo droplet the runner reaches Odoo over internal DNS; the six
# Hurl journeys (health / catalog / cart / checkout-canary / ghost) all hit this
# base. Tenants are the header scopes grove_headless resolves company from.
SYNTHETIC_ODOO_BASE="http://odoo:8069"
SYNTHETIC_TENANTS="goldberry,ggg,nursery"

# ── checkout-canary — Odoo XML-RPC (GATED: CEO ok to seed prod + Josh key) ────
# canary.py seeds the $0 SYNTHETIC-CANARY product + resolves the variant +
# sweeps its draft orders over XML-RPC on the droplet (ODOO_XMLRPC_URL internal).
# SYNTHETIC_ODOO_API_KEY doubles as the /orders HTTP bearer AND the XML-RPC
# password — it MUST be scoped to a least-privilege Odoo user (create
# product/order + unlink sale.order; nothing else). Unset => canary.py no-ops
# even with SYNTHETIC_CANARY_ENABLED=true (fail-closed). ODOO_DB is `odoo` on prod.
#
# BLOCKER (issue GOL-2325 gate a+b): do NOT enable this until (1) the CEO has
# OK'd seeding a $0 canary SKU + test partner into PROD Odoo, and (2) Josh has
# minted the least-privilege Odoo user + API key and stored it at the ref below.
# GOL-2325 (2026-09-29): held at "false" DELIBERATELY. The two refs below were
# committed as literal `REPLACE_ITEM_ID` placeholders, and `op run` fails the
# ENTIRE render on an unresolvable ref — so these placeholders blocked not just
# the canary but every read-only journey too. They stay commented until Josh
# mints the item (gate b); the read-only tier ships in the meantime.
# TO ENABLE once gates (a)+(b) clear: set ENABLED="true", uncomment both refs,
# and replace REPLACE_ITEM_ID with the real `Grove Prod` item id. (Verified
# 2026-09-29: no synthetic-canary item exists in `Grove Prod` yet.)
SYNTHETIC_CANARY_ENABLED="false"
ODOO_XMLRPC_URL="http://odoo:8069"
ODOO_DB="odoo"
# ODOO_LOGIN="op://Grove Prod/REPLACE_ITEM_ID/synthetic_canary_odoo_login"
# SYNTHETIC_ODOO_API_KEY="op://Grove Prod/REPLACE_ITEM_ID/synthetic_canary_odoo_api_key"

# ── ghost-content — per-tenant Content API (read-only keys) ──────────────────
# Prod Ghost lives on the shared blogs droplet behind the CF-proxied blog.*
# hosts. Provide a read-only Content API key per tenant; a missing pair just
# skips that tenant's ghost journey (run.py). Content keys are low-sensitivity
# but still vault-held so they never sit in a committed .env on the droplet.
# GOL-2325 (2026-09-29): the three per-tenant refs here were `REPLACE_ITEM_ID`
# placeholders, and the runbook listed "store the 3 Ghost content keys" as a
# human gate. That gate was already satisfied: the vault ALREADY holds all four
# tenants' read-only Content API keys as one JSON object at
# `Grove Infra/ghost_content_keys_tf_json` (keys hub/goldberry/ggg/nursery —
# verified 2026-09-29), because Terraform consumes them that way. An `op run`
# env-file cannot index into a JSON value, so we inject the whole bundle and
# run.py picks the tenant out (_ghost_key_from_bundle). No human step needed.
# A GHOST_KEY_<TENANT> var still wins over the bundle if one is ever set.
SYNTHETIC_GHOST_ENABLED="true"
GHOST_CONTENT_KEYS_JSON="op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/ghost_content_keys_tf_json"
GHOST_URL_GOLDBERRY="https://blog.goldberrygrove.farm"
GHOST_URL_GGG="https://blog.woodworkingeorge.com"
GHOST_URL_NURSERY="https://blog.atthegrovenursery.com"

# ── OTel Collector postgresql receiver (app-plane, GOL-335 — REQUIRED) ────────
# The app-plane collector merges config.postgres.yaml; leaving these unset FAILS
# collector startup. Read-only otel_monitor user against the prod Managed PG.
# GOL-2325 (2026-09-29): was `.../pg_prod_private_host_port`, which DOES NOT
# EXIST in the vault (only pg_qa_l3_private_host / pg_qa_l3_port are there) —
# another unresolvable ref that failed the whole render, and this one also fails
# collector STARTUP if unset. The private hostname is not a secret (it resolves
# only inside our VPC and grants nothing without the credentials below); taken
# from the DO API for grove-prod-pg, same VPC as the droplet. The monitor user
# + password below are real refs and DO resolve.
POSTGRES_ENDPOINT="private-grove-prod-pg-do-user-1201760-0.g.db.ondigitalocean.com:25060"
POSTGRES_MONITOR_USER="op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/pg_otel_monitor_user"
POSTGRES_MONITOR_PASSWORD="op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/pg_otel_monitor_password"

# ── Availability / SSL probe overrides — PROD public hostnames (non-secret) ──
# probes.py falls back to the monitors.json defaults for any unset var, and
# those defaults are LOCAL-DEV targets (http://host.docker.internal:3000-3003,
# ghost-goldberry:2368, ...). On prod they resolve to nothing, so leaving these
# commented does NOT mean "no probes" — it means 11 frontend + 3 ghost monitors
# report DOWN and page Discord. These overrides are therefore REQUIRED on prod,
# not optional.
#
# UNCOMMENTED 2026-09-29 (GOL-2325). Every path below was verified live against
# production from the agent plane on that date — all HTTP 200, and each site's
# monitors.json `expected_keyword` present in the body:
#   hub root+journal        200  "Gather at the Grove"    ✓
#   goldberry root/shop/blog 200  "Goldberry Grove"        ✓
#   ggg root/shop/blog       200  "George"                 ✓  (title: "George George George Woodworking")
#   nursery root/shop/blog   200  "At The Grove Nursery"   ✓
#   blog.* /ghost/api/admin/site/ ×3  200                  ✓
# TLS on all 7 hostnames was valid, earliest notAfter 2026-11-01 (Google Trust
# Services via the CF edge) — comfortably clear of the 10-10 deadline, so the
# ssl-* monitors will not fire on day one.
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_HUB_ROOT="https://gatheringatthegrove.com/"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_HUB_JOURNAL="https://gatheringatthegrove.com/journal"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_GOLDBERRY_ROOT="https://goldberrygrove.farm/"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_GOLDBERRY_SHOP="https://goldberrygrove.farm/shop"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_GOLDBERRY_BLOG="https://goldberrygrove.farm/blog"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_GGG_ROOT="https://woodworkingeorge.com/"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_GGG_SHOP="https://woodworkingeorge.com/shop"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_GGG_BLOG="https://woodworkingeorge.com/blog"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_NURSERY_ROOT="https://atthegrovenursery.com/"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_NURSERY_SHOP="https://atthegrovenursery.com/shop"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_NURSERY_BLOG="https://atthegrovenursery.com/blog"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_GHOST_GOLDBERRY_ADMIN="https://blog.goldberrygrove.farm/ghost/api/admin/site/"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_GHOST_GGG_ADMIN="https://blog.woodworkingeorge.com/ghost/api/admin/site/"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_URL_GHOST_NURSERY_ADMIN="https://blog.atthegrovenursery.com/ghost/api/admin/site/"
# Postgres TCP probe — prod Managed PG private host + DO port. The vault ref
# this used (`pg_prod_private_host`) DOES NOT EXIST; same non-secret private
# hostname as POSTGRES_ENDPOINT above. Enabled so postgres-tcp stops probing the
# monitors.json local default.
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_HOST_POSTGRES_TCP="private-grove-prod-pg-do-user-1201760-0.g.db.ondigitalocean.com"
SYNTHETIC_MONITOR_PORT_POSTGRES_TCP="25060"

# ── Keep + Discord (only needed if this env-file also runs setup-monitoring.py's
#    Keep bootstrap; the obs-plane deploy usually owns that). Uncomment to reuse.
# NOTE (GOL-2325, verified 2026-09-29): the three refs below were ALL wrong
# names and would fail the render if uncommented as-is. In the vault the field
# is `obs_keep_webhook_token` (not `keep_webhook_token`), and there is NO
# `discord_webhook_warning` / `discord_webhook_critical` — only a single
# `discord_webhook_url`. GOL-2329 (Keep -> Discord) wants severity-split
# routing, so either two new webhook fields get minted or route-critical.yml /
# route-warning.yml both target the one URL and lean on Keep's own severity in
# the payload. Tracked on GOL-2329; corrected names left commented here.
# KEEP_WEBHOOK_TOKEN="op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/obs_keep_webhook_token"
# DISCORD_WEBHOOK_WARNING="op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/discord_webhook_url"
# DISCORD_WEBHOOK_CRITICAL="op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/discord_webhook_url"
